{
  "_comment": "Milestone F tool permission profiles (bd: shode-roadmap/C-F1, ROADMAP-runtime-10.md SS 11.1). Read by scripts/permission-check.sh + CI drift check ONLY -- this file is NEVER preloaded into any agent context (token/run budget rule in the C-F1 brief). Two layers per agent: (1) tool_grants = FACTS transcribed from each agents/<name>.md `tools:` frontmatter -- platform-enforced, a subagent without the tool physically cannot perform the action; CI asserts the `tools` array here matches the frontmatter verbatim, so this file cannot drift from reality. (2) policy = least-privilege caps NARROWER than the tool grants (write_code / deploy / deploy_prod), derived from capability ownership in references/registry/capabilities.json -- deny-by-default, advisory until a hook calls permission-check.sh on the hot path (see 15-sentinel-runtime-security.md SS enforceability). No agent is granted anything here that its frontmatter does not already allow.",
  "_derivation": {
    "write_files": "true iff `tools:` contains Write or Edit",
    "run_commands": "true iff `tools:` contains Bash",
    "spawn": "true iff `tools:` contains Task (see delegation.json for who may spawn whom)",
    "network": "true iff `tools:` contains WebSearch or WebFetch",
    "write_code": "policy layer: true only where capabilities.json ownership includes producing code/test/infra files (production-code=developer, code-review incl. unit test+mutation=code-reviewer, integration-test=qa-engineer, devops-deploy incl. CI/IaC/observability=devops-engineer, security-threat-model incl. security config+fixtures=security-engineer). Everyone else writes ARTIFACTS (outputs/**, docs, specs, design tokens), not code -- Write/Edit grant alone does not imply write_code",
    "deploy": "policy layer: devops-deploy capability owner only (capabilities.json: devops-engineer, sole owner -- zero overlap rule)",
    "deploy_prod": "policy layer: subset of deploy; ROADMAP SS 11.1 names devops-engineer as the only deploy_prod=true role. permission-check.sh additionally requires the run_commands tool grant as a prerequisite, so a policy typo alone can never widen access"
  },
